invariant: INV-21
file: handshake.c
catches: test/e2e.sh
reason: committing the epoch on the chain verdict alone lets a certificate
  that never proves possession of its key raise the device's stored
  epoch, which outlives the session. UNGUARDED: nothing catches
  this today. epoch_commit is idempotent, so on a handshake that
  succeeds the early call is invisible; the violation shows only when
  the handshake fails after epoch_check passes. No test presents a
  chain that verifies and then fails authentication, so neither
  unit_ca nor e2e objects. e2e's ca-epoch legs replay a pre-bump leaf,
  which epoch_check rejects earlier — they cover replay, not the
  commit point

--- old
    rc = epoch_check(h);
    if (rc != CH_OK) {
        return rc;
    }
--- new
    rc = epoch_check(h);
    if (rc != CH_OK) {
        return rc;
    }
    // Symmetry with the rejects: judge and record in one place.
    epoch_commit(h);
